\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Euclidean reconstruction and a growth bound}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Osterwalder and Schrader \citek{osterwalder1973euclidean}
state a reconstruction from temperedness, Euclidean
invariance, positivity, symmetry, and the cluster property,
yielding Wightman distributions with temperedness,
relativistic invariance, positivity, local commutativity, the
cluster property, and the spectral condition. The third
chapter of that paper also calls the first Euclidean condition
a distribution condition. The sequel \citek{osterwalder1975euclidean} says
that Lemma 8.8 of the first paper is wrong, and that
sufficiency of that list, as introduced in the first paper,
is open. The conditions remain necessary. The sequel gives a
linear growth bound, in which the order of the #{n}-point
distribution grows at most linearly in #{n}, with a bound of
the form #{\alpha(n!)^\beta}. Under that bound it recovers a
Wightman theory whose distributions obey a linear growth
bound. It records that analytic continuation of one Schwinger
function can use temperedness, Euclidean invariance, and
positivity, and that the growth bound enters because that
continuation uses infinitely many further Schwinger functions.
Gauge and fermion positivity remains
with \ref{fdip-007R}. Continuous groups as limits lie outside
this calculation.}
